<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Learning to Manage Time – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Study Habits for Success</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Learning to Manage Time</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Evaluate arguments in opinion pieces. Reading: Make basic inferences or predictions about a text content from headings, titles, or headlines.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can evaluate and reflect on arguments in study habits articles and make connections to personal academic experiences (e.g., "The article suggests using checklists. I've tried this strategy, and it has helped me stay organized."). Can make inferences about the main focus of a section based on its title and supporting text (e.g., "The section titled 'Boost Your Focus' likely discusses concentration techniques such as minimizing distractions and using a timer during study sessions."). Can predict the type of advice provided in a section on time management by examining headings and context clues (e.g., "The heading 'Tips for Daily Progress' suggests that the section will include steps for creating a daily routine and using calendars or to-do lists.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying images of different study environments (e.g., organized desk, messy desk, student using a planner, student looking stressed). Students are asked to briefly describe what they see and connect it to study habits and time management. Teacher poses WH-questions: "What are your biggest challenges when trying to manage your study time?" or "How do you usually organize your assignments?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models an answer using past simple and present perfect structures and target vocabulary: "I used to procrastinate a lot with big assignments, but recently, I have developed a better routine by using a digital planner to track my goals. This has improved my productivity significantly." The teacher stresses the main verb in forms like "have developed."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you 'procrastinate,' do you finish tasks quickly or delay them?" (Expected answer: "Delay them.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does an 'effective strategy' help you achieve results, or not?" (Expected answer: "Yes, it helps achieve results.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher will also model the pronunciation of /sk/ in "skill" and /st/ in "study," emphasizing the difference.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Study Habits for Success."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive a printed or digital copy of an opinion piece titled "Master Your Time, Master Your Grades: Effective Study Habits for High Schoolers." The article includes sections with headings such as "Prioritize Your Tasks," "Boost Your Focus," and "Reflect on Your Progress," using vocabulary like <i>manage, set goals, improve, organize, achieve, productivity, consistent, focused, skills, achievements, progress, routines, assignments</i>. The text also incorporates present perfect for achievements and past simple for reflection.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading comprehension for gist, specific information, and making inferences based on text structure and content.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Reading focus):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pre-reading Prediction:</b> Students first read the main title and all headings. In pairs, they predict what kind of advice or information each section will contain. For example, "What do you think the section 'Boost Your Focus' will discuss?" or "The heading 'Tips for Daily Progress' suggests..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students quickly read the entire article (1-2 minutes) to identify the author's main argument about why time management is essential for academic success. They then select the best summary sentence from a provided list.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching Headings to Content:</b> Students match each heading from the article with a short summary sentence (provided by the teacher) that captures the main idea of that specific section. This helps them practice making inferences about the main focus of a section.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Deep reading comprehension, identifying explicit and implicit information, evaluating arguments, practicing vocabulary (nouns: <i>study, habits, focus, time, goals, grades, progress, learning, achievements, skills, routines, assignments, strategies, productivity, improvement, management, results, effort, success, inspiration</i>; verbs: <i>manage, procrastinate, improve, develop, reflect, suggest, set (goals), practice, study, focus, achieve, organize, complete, work on, discuss, inspire, evaluate, prioritize, accomplish</i>; adjectives: <i>effective, frequent, productive, focused, essential, improved, organized, consistent</i>) and grammar (Present perfect for achievements, Past simple for reflecting, WH-questions).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Vocabulary in Context):</b> Students read specific paragraphs again. They are given a list of sentences with gaps from the article and a word bank (containing target vocabulary words). They fill in the blanks, ensuring they understand the vocabulary in the context of time management strategies. For example: "Setting clear ______ (goals) is an essential first step for effective time _______ (management)." The teacher reviews answers, modeling pronunciation of /s/ in "success" and /t/ in "type" when discussing words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled (Identifying Arguments & Support):</b> The teacher provides a set of statements representing arguments or key advice from the article. Students reread the article to locate the specific sentences or phrases that support each statement. They highlight or write down the evidence. For example: "Argument: Taking short breaks can significantly boost your concentration. Supporting Text: 'Research has shown that frequent, short breaks actually improve your focus and prevent burnout, leading to better results.'"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Evaluating and Inferring):</b> In pairs, students discuss two key arguments from the article. They evaluate whether they agree with these arguments and why, making connections to their personal academic experiences. They use target grammar and pragmatic functions: "The article suggests using checklists to organize tasks. I have tried this strategy, and it has helped me stay organized," or "The author argues that early study sessions are more productive. I used to study late at night, but I might try working on my assignments before dinner instead." They also infer the author's purpose in certain sections. Teacher monitors and provides feedback on pronunciation of -ed endings (e.g., /d/ in "improved," /t/ in "accomplished," /ɪd/ in "suggested").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Study Habits for Success."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Discussion and Short Oral Report.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students form small groups (3-4 students). Their task is to collaboratively discuss and evaluate the arguments and strategies presented in the article. Each group must identify: </p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Two study strategies from the article they consider most <i>effective</i> and explain why, relating them to personal experiences (e.g., "We believe setting clear goals is effective because I have achieved better grades since I started doing it.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One strategy they *used to* follow that was not very helpful, and what they *might* do instead based on the article's suggestions. (e.g., "I used to procrastinate on my assignments, but the article suggests prioritizing tasks. I might try that.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They should use WH-questions to facilitate their discussion: "What have you done to change your study routine?" or "How could this strategy improve your learning?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">After 7-8 minutes of discussion, each group chooses a spokesperson to present their findings to the class in a short (1-2 minute) oral report. The teacher emphasizes collaborative discussions on effective study strategies, encouraging students to negotiate plans and share experiences, providing concrete examples of time management techniques adapted to their cultural and social norms (e.g., "Let's create a study schedule where we review one subject per day and take 10-minute breaks after 40 minutes of studying. We should use the Pomodoro technique...").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist for group discussions and oral reports.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Groups present their evaluated study strategies and personal connections to the class. The teacher assesses their ability to critically engage with the text and use the target language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to accurately identify and evaluate arguments from the reading material.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., <i>effective, manage, prioritize, focus, achieve, progress</i>) and grammar (Present Perfect, Past Simple).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence in expressing ideas and justifications.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborative participation and negotiation within the group.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, especially of -ed endings and contrastive sounds.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions individually on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most convincing argument in the article for you, and why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one specific study habit you have improved or plan to improve after reading this article?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did reading this opinion piece challenge or change your perspective on managing time for studying?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new strategies for making inferences or evaluating arguments from a text have you learned or practiced today?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students evaluate arguments and make inferences from the text? (e.g., "The pre-reading prediction activity effectively activated prior knowledge and set the purpose for reading.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students to better grasp evaluating arguments in opinion pieces in the next reading lesson? (e.g., "Some students struggled to distinguish between main arguments and supporting details; I need to provide more explicit sentence frames for identifying evidence.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How effectively did students use the target vocabulary and grammar structures (Present Perfect, Past Simple) in their discussions?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "Today we read about and evaluated effective study habits. Next lesson, we will focus on actively speaking and interacting in pairs to discuss and propose personalized study schedules and time management routines, building on the strategies we've just read about."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>